{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"disparaged-parodistic-shrew","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# State-based echo suppression + LWW conflict safety\n\n## Context / Problem\nBecause the DC codec is one-way and lossy, loop prevention cannot rely on `decode(encode(x))==x`. Both clients must instead suppress echo by **state**: compare the current Jira value against the last-synced baseline the reconciler already keeps (ADR 0026, `binding_store.py:102`; `_BASELINE_FIELDS` at line 108; `set_baseline` at line 459). We must also not silently destroy either side's edits under concurrency, and any state we add must respect epic `0303`'s churn discipline (a per-pass timestamp there caused the tickets-store bloat this feature must not regress).\n\n## Approach\nDrive body sync from a three-way state — `baseline` (last-synced Jira canonical form), `local` (rebar body), `remote` (current Jira value) — normalized through a **compare-only** `norm()` before diffing. Outbound fires only when `local` differs from `baseline` (rebar-originated change); a value equal to `baseline` is our own echo and is skipped. **The baseline is the value OBSERVED AFTER push** — after pushing a changed field, GET it back and store Jira's canonicalized form as the new baseline (one extra GET, only on change) — which neutralizes Jira's own storage canonicalization so a benign re-canonicalization is never mistaken for a human edit. When both `local` and `remote` diverged from `baseline` (concurrent edit), apply last-write-wins to pick the winner but always emit a detectable conflict signal so nothing is silently lost. For the rich layer, store a **change-gated `rich_sha = sha256(rendered_wire)[:16]` (8 bytes) inline** in the binding (proven 0.07 KB/version stable, identical to baseline; a per-pass timestamp was proven to reproduce 0303's 12.62 KB anti-pattern) so a re-render is only pushed when the wire actually changed. `norm()` stays comparison-only and never writes back (a normalizing write would churn git and re-trigger gates); it absorbs only whitespace and pandoc `<!-- -->` list-separator injects — proven to mask 0 real content changes.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py` — inline change-gated `rich_sha` beside the existing baseline; observed-after-push baseline update.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py` — compare-only `norm()` and the three-way diff/LWW arbitration.\n- `tests/` — echo, conflict, and no-churn tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Self-echo: a rebar-originated body change propagates exactly once and a second reconcile pass pushes nothing (0 echo-back).\n- Concurrent: both sides edit the same body; the pass records a conflict and applies LWW without discarding the loser silently (regression asserting the 799/799-detected invariant over the corpus sample).\n- No-churn: a pass with no body change updates 0 `rich_sha` entries and writes no timestamp; asserted by the git-delta cost harness (≤0.07 KB/version stable).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Outbound fires only on a rebar-originated change and the baseline stored is the value observed after push; verified by `test_echo_observed_after_push` (a benign Jira re-canonicalization does not re-sync).\n- [ ] A concurrent both-sides edit is detected and flagged under LWW with the losing side never silently destroyed; verified by `test_concurrent_conflict_detected` (799/799 over the corpus sample).\n- [ ] The rich layer stores a change-gated inline 8-byte `rich_sha` and no per-pass timestamp, changing 0 entries on a no-op pass; verified by the git-delta cost harness reproducing the ≤0.07 KB/version stable profile.\n- [ ] `norm()` is comparison-only (never written back) and masks 0 real content changes; verified by `test_norm_masks_nothing` over the adversarial pair set.","id":"3388-96e8-e14e-4221","parent_id":"708d-0767-1a51-4928","priority":2,"tags":["rich-text"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"State-based echo suppression + LWW conflict safety"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785800295896956001,"uuid":"0741c7de-e2db-443d-bc92-cff2fcf0dd80"}